Title:
EDR Analysis: Leveraging Fake DLLs, Guard Pages, and VEH for Enhanced Detection

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post reverse engineers an unnamed EDR’s unconventional user-mode detection chain that goes beyond typical inline hooks/ETW/callbacks.  
- The EDR modifies the PEB loader module lists to insert manually mapped “fake” `ntdll.dll`/`kernel32.dll` entries early in load order, with RX regions marked as guard pages.  
- Accessing these fake modules (e.g., via common PEB-walking techniques used by shellcode/syscall resolvers) triggers `STATUS_GUARD_PAGE_VIOLATION (0x80000001)`, which is handled by an EDR-registered Vectored Exception Handler (VEH) to gain control and potentially block/kill execution.  
- The author demonstrates how `DllBase` (offset `0x30`) is overwritten to point to the fake module while `OriginalBase` (offset `0xF8`) still points to the real module, breaking naive SSN/exports resolution logic.  
- It also shows ways to validate VEH usage via PEB `CrossProcessFlags` and debugging `RtlAddVectoredExceptionHandler` call stacks.  
- Useful for red teamers/malware researchers debugging EDR behavior and for defenders understanding anti-evasion traps targeting PEB-walking loaders.

Technical Focus:
- PEB/Loader list manipulation (`PEB_LDR_DATA`, `InLoadOrderModuleList`, `LDR_DATA_TABLE_ENTRY`)
- Manual mapping of system DLLs and module masquerading (“fake DLLs”)
- Page Guard / Guard Page Hooking (`PAGE_GUARD`, `STATUS_GUARD_PAGE_VIOLATION`)
- Vectored Exception Handling (VEH) registration and detection (`AddVectoredExceptionHandler`, `RtlAddVectoredExceptionHandler`, `CrossProcessFlags`)
- Impact on syscall resolution techniques (dynamic SSN lookup via PEB walk + EAT parsing)

Use Cases:
- Analyze and reproduce EDR user-mode “trap” mechanisms in lab environments
- Debug why PEB-walking loaders/syscall resolvers crash or get terminated under certain EDRs
- Build more robust module base discovery logic (e.g., avoid `DllBase` traps, validate module identity)
- Develop defensive detections for anomalous module lists, guard pages on mapped images, and unexpected VEH registration

Keywords:
EDR, Windows internals, PEB, PEB_LDR_DATA, LDR_DATA_TABLE_ENTRY, InLoadOrderModuleList, InMemoryOrderModuleList, ntdll.dll, kernel32.dll, manual mapping, module masquerading, PAGE_GUARD, guard page hooking, STATUS_GUARD_PAGE_VIOLATION, VEH, Vectored Exception Handler, AddVectoredExceptionHandler, RtlAddVectoredExceptionHandler, CrossProcessFlags, syscall resolution, SSN, EAT parsing, WinDbg, x64dbg